what are you guys doing
the rest of the day?

00:19:00.617 --> 00:19:01.519
Right.

00:19:01.519 --> 00:19:02.352
And we were like,

00:19:02.352 --> 00:19:03.300
well, we're making more GMM.

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:04.133
Like we're doing,

00:19:04.133 --> 00:19:06.050
we're doing what we do every day,

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:06.883
that we're shooting GMM.

00:19:06.883 --> 00:19:07.890
So we didn't get,

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:09.660
we never hung out after the first time,

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:10.860
which is like a,

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:11.693
I was like, man,

00:19:11.693 --> 00:19:13.475
that's such a big regret.

00:19:13.475 --> 00:19:14.520
I was like,

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:16.380
I'm not gonna make that mistake again.

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:17.663
Yeah.

00:19:17.663 --> 00:19:18.496
If there's an invitation.

00:19:18.496 --> 00:19:19.648
Right.

00:19:19.648 --> 00:19:21.450
So then, you know,

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.314
we set the night we were gonna hang out

00:19:23.314 --> 00:19:25.590
and don't make this my story.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:26.423
We're both there, so, I mean,

00:19:26.423 --> 00:19:27.256
we just kind of like,

00:19:27.256 --> 00:19:28.089
fill in the gaps.

00:19:28.089 --> 00:19:30.300
But yeah,

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:31.133
he was like,

00:19:31.133 --> 00:19:32.520
whatever you guys want to do,

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:33.900
if you wanna come by the studio.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:34.733
And so when he,

00:19:34.733 --> 00:19:37.470
once he mentioned the stew,

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:40.113
as he put it, I was like, yes,

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:43.050
that's what I want to do.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:45.064
I yeah, let's hang out.

00:19:45.064 --> 00:19:49.290
So we go over there to the studio

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:52.020
and we meet Lou, his producer,

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:54.690
and there's just a couple other friends

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:55.523
of his hanging out,

00:19:55.523 --> 00:20:00.470
but it's like, one room, very dark,

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.680
with like the console and
then back to the left,

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:07.230
there's like the booth with the window.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.060
So it's a pretty intimate space.

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.500
And we go in there and we're just,

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:11.490
you know, it's,

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:14.400
we had, you see how much fun

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.080
we had on the show.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:16.913
You know, it's,

00:20:16.913 --> 00:20:18.690
it always blew my mind

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:22.980
about how much of a fan he was and is,

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:24.540
of GMM.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:26.220
So it's like,

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.350
and you saw how it was
in Good Mythical More,

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:29.400
we were just like talking about

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:31.336
the gas station pills and like,

00:20:31.336 --> 00:20:33.450
so when we were setting
up, going to hang out,

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:34.901
like we were making jokes about

00:20:34.901 --> 00:20:36.060
I don't want to have any

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:37.680
of those gas station pills.

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:39.910
There was a lot of
laughter the entire time.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:41.520
Yeah, we just kind of picked up--

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.980
We making each other laugh the whole time.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:44.813
And then--

00:20:44.813 --> 00:20:45.936
Playing music.

00:20:45.936 --> 00:20:47.490
We were connecting over our love of music

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:48.840
and specifically country music.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:49.860
So there was a lot of there.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.390
In fact, there was one time,

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:52.500
there was a period of time

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:54.630
where there was like a Hank Junior

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:56.370
versus Hank Senior Battle,

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.620
where we would pick a Hank Junior song

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:00.330
and he would pick a Hank Senior song,

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:01.380
and we would play them.

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:04.860
Like a verses battle, which was hilarious.

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:06.390
And that lasted, I mean--

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.290
Hours.

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:09.210
We were there a very long time.

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:12.782
And just kind of getting
the story of like--

00:21:12.782 --> 00:21:13.800
And we don't stay, we don't do that.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.710
Your bedtime is 9:30.

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:16.710
I mean, I do stay up till midnight

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:18.450
on a relatively regular basis,

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:19.950
but this was,

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:22.380
as we crept into the wee
hours of the morning,

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:25.050
I was like, I guess this
is just how he rolls.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:26.286
Oh yeah.

00:21:26.286 --> 00:21:27.360
It's like he goes to bed--

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:28.193
He's nocturnal.

00:21:28.193 --> 00:21:29.280
When the sun comes up.

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:31.470
And yeah,

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.900
I'm so fascinated about
the creative process

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:35.010
that I couldn't help myself

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:35.970
but just kind of dig into

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:38.790
like his relationship with Lou

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:40.410
and like how much,

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:42.506
how they collaborate and,

00:21:42.506 --> 00:21:44.276
you know, they were playing,

00:21:44.276 --> 00:21:48.450
playing like preliminary tracks

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:50.731
of things that then became

00:21:50.731 --> 00:21:54.272
like massive hits and I was just like,

00:21:54.272 --> 00:21:57.270
I was just like a kid in a candy store.

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:58.260
Like I couldn't sit down.

00:21:58.260 --> 00:21:59.220
It was pretty obvious.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:00.320
I couldn't sit down.

00:22:01.525 --> 00:22:02.460
Like I'm not, yeah.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:03.510
And I'm like, you know what?

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:04.560
I don't...

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:08.160
I am, if I'm gonna fanboy,

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:09.624
I'm just gonna do it.

00:22:09.624 --> 00:22:11.100
You know, it's like over,

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.480
especially if it's over the music,

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:13.410
like I'm not,

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:14.640
I think he understood that

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:18.690
I was like fanboying over
like the creative process

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:22.380
and the music and not the fact that like,

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.480
oh my God, it's so cool to meet you.

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:25.320
You know, it's a,

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:26.820
in my mind it's a different thing.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:27.981
So it's like,

00:22:27.981 --> 00:22:31.320
to like really connect with something

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:33.540
that an artist is making to the point

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:34.373
where it's like,

00:22:34.373 --> 00:22:35.700
if they're gonna play something for you,

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:38.284
you're going to get up and shake your ass.

00:22:38.284 --> 00:22:40.890
That's just the decision I made.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:42.862
Like dance like nobody's watching, right?

00:22:42.862 --> 00:22:44.027
And it's not,

00:22:44.027 --> 00:22:45.540
it doesn't have to be
everybody's decision.

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:47.610
Well there's a reason
that there's two of us.

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:48.443
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:22:48.443 --> 00:22:50.190
I appreciate that.

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:52.650
So yeah, I was, I mean,

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.030
and at certain moments, yeah,

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:56.790
I was kind of in like
music journalism mode

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:57.750
where it's like, how,

00:22:57.750 --> 00:22:59.526
give me, I have to understand.

00:22:59.526 --> 00:23:02.474
And they were, you know,

00:23:02.474 --> 00:23:04.553
I think they were both very

00:23:04.553 --> 00:23:07.537
into the conversation
because they were like,

00:23:07.537 --> 00:23:08.790
"Oh, play him this,

00:23:08.790 --> 00:23:10.892
play him that, play him this demo."

00:23:10.892 --> 00:23:13.500
And then, you know,

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.108
at the time we were talking about

00:23:15.108 --> 00:23:18.839
the neon moon cover that we made.

00:23:18.839 --> 00:23:19.672
Oh yeah.

00:23:19.672 --> 00:23:21.330
For the Mythical Society.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:22.890
'Cause we were playing
so much country music.

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:24.430
We were talking about that.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:29.250
And I think at one point I said,

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:31.140
or one of us said like,

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:33.150
well I'll make a deal with you,

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:36.300
we'll play this song for you,

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:38.010
under one condition.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:38.910
And he's like, "What?"

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.823
And I was like, that you play a song,

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.700
like give us a sneak peek of a song

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:46.470
off your album.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:47.835
'Cause I know it's,

00:23:47.835 --> 00:23:50.340
I know it's coming out soon,

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:51.730
that you finished it.

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:52.810
And he was like, well,

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:54.210
I wanna play you the whole album.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:55.860
Like he was planning on doing it anyway.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.150
Yeah, he was like,

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:59.940
I, yeah, I'm not gonna play a song.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:03.030
I'll only play it if you
listen to the whole thing.

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.040
And I'll never forget that moment,

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:06.030
'cause I was like,

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:09.060
I was already like the
kid in the candy store

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.650
and then I'm like,

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:12.427
we're looking at each
other and we're like,

00:24:12.427 --> 00:24:14.070
"Yes, this is amazing."

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:15.360
So we just,

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:19.380
we sat there and we just listened

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:24.120
to the entire completed, pretty,

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:24.990
it was, I mean,

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:27.990
every song was sequenced, finished--

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.310
All but one song.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:30.804
Fully baked.

00:24:30.804 --> 00:24:31.813
And--

00:24:31.813 --> 00:24:33.237
I remember sitting there

00:24:33.237 --> 00:24:34.612
and this is when it hit me

00:24:34.612 --> 00:24:36.750
and why it stands out.

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.700
And I would say,

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:42.690
if you're talking about
exact slices of time,

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:43.980
like one moment,

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.170
I do recognize why this
is higher on your list.

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:48.521
And it's just, you'll understand why,

00:24:48.521 --> 00:24:49.980
there's other things

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:51.720
that are higher on the list later,

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:53.520
but this moment,

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:55.386
this slice of life

00:24:55.386 --> 00:24:57.960
when we were a few tracks in

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:00.460
and we were like listening
with our eyes closed

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:04.920
or I was listening with my eyes closed.

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:06.390
This is Twelve Carat Toothache.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:07.560
So it's like, if you've listened to it,

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:08.940
you know that a number of places,

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:10.260
it gets pretty moody.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:11.460
Like it's not,

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:13.260
it's not all party anthem type stuff.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:16.530
And then I would open my eyes

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:17.714
and realize that,

00:25:17.714 --> 00:25:20.310
Post Malone is sitting next to me.

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:22.260
It's great, it was wild.

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:23.730
Listening to this album, and again,

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:24.750
we just don't have a lot

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:26.460
of experiences like
this because we're not,

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:27.293
like, we're not part,

00:25:27.293 --> 00:25:28.860
we don't try to be a part of a scene.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.360
We don't like, we're just,

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:33.900
our lives are strikingly normal

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.010
in almost every way,

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.140
except for these little moments.

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:39.210
And I was just like,

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:40.860
this is a memory.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:42.810
I was like, this is a special moment.

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:44.010
Yeah.

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:44.843
So we,

00:25:44.843 --> 00:25:45.676
and we listened to the entire thing,

00:25:45.676 --> 00:25:48.060
incredible experience.

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:49.260
Also, they play it so loud,

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:50.580
I felt like it was like literally,

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:52.712
like shaking my spleen,

00:25:52.712 --> 00:25:56.892
and then it's like literally 3:30 AM.

00:25:56.892 --> 00:25:58.273
And he is like--

00:25:58.273 --> 00:26:01.620
Well, let me, okay.

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:02.453
What did he say?

00:26:02.453 --> 00:26:03.286
He was like,

00:26:03.286 --> 00:26:06.750
"Oh, aren't we supposed
to record something?"

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:07.890
Yeah.

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:09.270
And earlier that night,

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:11.242
when we were talking about,

00:26:11.242 --> 00:26:13.380
like our favorite artists

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:15.240
and we were talking about Sturgill Simpson

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:16.190
and all this stuff,

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:19.630
we also started talking
about the Fleet Foxes.

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:20.463
Yeah.

00:26:20.463 --> 00:26:21.960
Which is, you know,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:24.870
from our top albums episode.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:27.693
Like we both, they're like,

00:26:27.693 --> 00:26:32.340
they're form, albums that
are like very formative

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:37.258
and like our musical lexicon, you know.

00:26:37.258 --> 00:26:40.754
And he's a huge fan.

00:26:40.754 --> 00:26:43.447
And so then he was like,

00:26:43.447 --> 00:26:45.845
"Play 'em the track."

00:26:45.845 --> 00:26:47.825
And then he was like, Robin,

00:26:47.825 --> 00:26:49.699
who is Fleet Fox's,

00:26:49.699 --> 00:26:52.320
came in here a few weeks ago

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:54.450
and we started working on something.

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:58.883
And then he plays a track and it's just,

00:26:58.883 --> 00:27:02.640
it's just vocalizations.

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:03.900
And then the beat drops

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:05.070
and then it's an instrumental.

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:08.160
So there's no vocals at all.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:09.120
And he plays that.

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:11.880
And that was like before we listened

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:13.350
to the whole album.

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:14.751
And it was just like,

00:27:14.751 --> 00:27:15.657
"Oh, this is just something

00:27:15.657 --> 00:27:16.590
that we created together."

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:17.820
And so I'm flipping out,

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:18.750
because it's like two

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:20.520
of my favorite artists working together

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:21.420
and one of 'em in the room

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:22.440
with me playing it,

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.540
and it's just a preliminary track,

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.760
which they didn't say
it would be anything.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:28.290
So then fast forward to,

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:29.587
at this point in the
evening when it's like,

00:27:29.587 --> 00:27:31.887
"Oh man, I forgot how I
was supposed to work."

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.110
So he goes into the booth

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:36.813
and then that's the track that he plays.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:39.660
And he, so this,

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:42.330
so this is the song that ended up being

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.441
the love hate letter to alcohol, right?

00:27:44.441 --> 00:27:45.274
Yeah.

00:27:45.274 --> 00:27:49.470
So he, this is, we saw his process,

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:51.640
which was essentially going into a booth

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:55.380
and Lou starts playing this track

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:58.200
and he just starts singing over it.

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:00.180
Literally just kind of ad-libbing

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.229
and making it up as he goes along.

00:28:02.229 --> 00:28:03.835
And it was like,

00:28:03.835 --> 00:28:05.370
he's creating this situation

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:06.900
where he's at the bar--

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:08.640
Yeah, like I woke up on the ground,

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:09.540
it's like I got my,

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:10.860
he started singing about his teeth

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:11.850
getting knocked out,

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:12.870
and then at one point,

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:15.975
like after about 10, 15 minutes

00:28:15.975 --> 00:28:17.640
of like riffing on stuff,

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:19.012
he comes out and he's like,

00:28:19.012 --> 00:28:21.097
"How many teeth?"

00:28:21.097 --> 00:28:22.027
"Google, how many teeth."

00:28:22.027 --> 00:28:24.240
"How many teeth are in the human mouth?"

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:25.499
And he had said 32.

00:28:25.499 --> 00:28:26.700
And we Googled it--

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:28.077
And it was 32.

00:28:28.077 --> 00:28:29.813
And yeah, I didn't, you Googled it

00:28:29.813 --> 00:28:30.960
and you were like, yes.

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:32.460
I have 32 teeth in my mouth.

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:33.293
The dentist told me,

00:28:33.293 --> 00:28:34.126
I'm like in like,

00:28:34.126 --> 00:28:36.300
a very low percentage of people

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:38.265
that still has their
wisdom teeth at my age.

00:28:38.265 --> 00:28:39.098
Oh.

00:28:39.098 --> 00:28:40.494
You don't have 32 teeth.

00:28:40.494 --> 00:28:41.327
So if you have--

00:28:41.327 --> 00:28:42.346
I don't know if he has his--

00:28:42.346 --> 00:28:43.428
If all of your teeth come in,

