00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:05.577
a fair amount of time together,

00:39:05.577 --> 00:39:09.180
and our thing is going
to see movies together

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:10.650
and we both love horror movies,

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:12.060
and we both have very similar taste

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:14.130
in music and movies in general.

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:15.830
And it's kind of our little thing.

00:39:17.628 --> 00:39:20.523
And so much so that, you know,

00:39:21.510 --> 00:39:23.580
ever since the ArcLight closed,

00:39:23.580 --> 00:39:26.670
all these theaters in
town are AMC theaters.

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:28.380
That's pretty much where you go is you,

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:29.520
if you're gonna go to a theater,

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:31.200
you go to an AMC theater

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:32.580
and they're constantly selling

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:35.400
this AMC Stubs membership, right?

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:36.450
Oh, did you get that?

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:39.510
So, every single time we show up

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:40.650
at the theater,

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:43.260
and we see the people in the yellow line

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:45.420
getting to the concessions
a little bit faster,

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:46.961
Shepherd's like,

00:39:46.961 --> 00:39:48.120
"Dad, we need to,

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:49.470
why aren't we Stubs members?"

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:50.973
And then we go in and we,

00:39:51.966 --> 00:39:52.959
and they have, then they give,

00:39:52.959 --> 00:39:54.690
they do it the ad for the Stubs membership

00:39:54.690 --> 00:39:56.523
and then they do the Nicole Kidman.

00:39:57.951 --> 00:39:59.190
You know, that wonderful little thing

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:00.690
that happens before AMC movies.

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:01.523
I just, we just,

00:40:01.523 --> 00:40:02.910
and people clap every time,

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:04.230
going to see a movie in LA,

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:05.730
I know this probably happens elsewhere,

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:07.530
but like in LA--

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:08.520
I haven't heard of claps

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:09.810
for that Nicole Kidman part.

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:11.242
Well you haven't been

00:40:11.242 --> 00:40:12.075
to the AMC Theater lately.

00:40:12.075 --> 00:40:12.908
Yes, I have.

00:40:12.908 --> 00:40:13.741
Every single time--

00:40:13.741 --> 00:40:14.837
What?

00:40:14.837 --> 00:40:15.836
Nicole Kidman comes on,

00:40:15.836 --> 00:40:16.669
everybody claps and giggles

00:40:16.669 --> 00:40:17.502
and says, "Oh yeah."

00:40:17.502 --> 00:40:18.335
And then,

00:40:18.335 --> 00:40:21.060
when she finishes, everybody claps.

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:21.893
Because,

00:40:21.893 --> 00:40:22.726
'cause there's,

00:40:22.726 --> 00:40:24.358
the thing I was talking to Shepherd about,

00:40:24.358 --> 00:40:25.710
is that like being in the entertainment

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:26.670
capital of the world,

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:27.870
and watching these movies

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:29.550
that are all pretty much, you know,

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:31.050
like there's a connection

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:32.670
between every movie in Los Angeles,

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:34.080
even if it was made somewhere else.

00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:36.240
And that so many people
are in the industry.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:37.830
And so there's this cynicism

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:40.020
and sort of insider mentality

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:41.100
to watching entertainment,

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:43.201
so that when like a bad
movie trailer comes up.

00:40:43.201 --> 00:40:44.333
Yeah.

00:40:44.333 --> 00:40:45.360
Like there, we just watched,

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:46.193
there was a trailer

00:40:46.193 --> 00:40:48.030
for a new movie called Plane.

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:48.863
Aha, yeah.

00:40:48.863 --> 00:40:49.911
Have you seen that?

00:40:49.911 --> 00:40:50.744
Yeah.

00:40:50.744 --> 00:40:51.870
And the whole place burst out laughing

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:53.100
as soon as it said Plane,

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.710
because it looks like the
worst movie ever made.

00:40:55.710 --> 00:40:57.150
Gerard Butler.

00:40:57.150 --> 00:40:58.890
And then it says Plane,

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:00.570
I just love kind watching movies

00:41:00.570 --> 00:41:01.403
in that atmosphere,

00:41:01.403 --> 00:41:02.640
and people stick around for the credits

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:03.968
because they're like,

00:41:03.968 --> 00:41:04.801
I might know somebody in those credits

00:41:04.801 --> 00:41:05.634
at the end, you know?

00:41:05.634 --> 00:41:06.570
Yeah.

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:08.040
But anyway, so--

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:10.020
Not a sponsor though, huh?

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:12.420
Well, literally right before

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:13.890
we went to our most recent movie,

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:15.390
which we went to see Glass Onion

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.220
on your recommendation and it was great.

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:18.150
Woo yeah.

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:19.770
We signed up for the membership,

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:20.880
not the top level,

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:22.440
where you get three movies a week.

00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:23.273
'Cause we can't,

00:41:23.273 --> 00:41:24.780
I can't see three movies a week.

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:26.100
I just got the one where we get like

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:28.530
the free upgrades on concessions,

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:29.880
we get the shorter line

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:32.691
and then we get points that we can use.

00:41:32.691 --> 00:41:33.524
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:41:33.524 --> 00:41:34.615
Brian knows what's up.

00:41:34.615 --> 00:41:35.448
This is a sponsor.

00:41:35.448 --> 00:41:36.281
Is this a sponsor?

00:41:36.281 --> 00:41:37.200
It's not a sponsor.

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:38.033
It could be.

00:41:38.033 --> 00:41:38.866
Anyway, it's been a real,

00:41:38.866 --> 00:41:40.179
we have this,

00:41:40.179 --> 00:41:41.850
Shepherd and I have a great relationship.

00:41:41.850 --> 00:41:43.170
It's tough to get Shepherd to talk,

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:45.600
unlike Locke who will
talk about everything,

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:47.840
but going to see a movie with Shepherd,

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:49.560
we talk about the movie

00:41:49.560 --> 00:41:50.940
all the way back home,

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:52.500
like that gets him talking.

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.470
He's like, well, this and that and man,

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:56.940
he's got all these
opinions about everything

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:58.830
and he really opens up and starts talking,

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:00.270
so that's been--

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:01.260
I love that.

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:03.210
And specifically like Smile.

00:42:03.210 --> 00:42:05.190
We talked about how it was
the perfect horror movie

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:06.840
until it,

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:08.610
they did what every horror movie does

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:10.680
and they show you the monster

00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:12.557
in a way that ends up being like,

00:42:12.557 --> 00:42:15.291
this isn't as good as I wanted it to be.

00:42:15.291 --> 00:42:20.010
Was there a point when you realized

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:22.770
this is my, this is the opportunity,

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:25.107
to stay with that buzzword?

00:42:25.107 --> 00:42:28.500
And I guess, was it
Smile when you were like,

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:29.550
when you're like--

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:30.900
To get the AMC Stubs?

00:42:30.900 --> 00:42:31.733
No, dude.

00:42:33.370 --> 00:42:34.203
No dude.

00:42:34.203 --> 00:42:35.036
This is the opportunity

00:42:35.036 --> 00:42:36.510
to finally bite the bullet.

00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:38.310
No, dude, when you're like,

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:40.860
this is, this is my,

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:44.850
this is my connection or
one of my connections.

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:46.278
This is like,

00:42:46.278 --> 00:42:49.050
this is an easy card I can play to like

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:50.160
get in with Shepherd

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:52.650
and find something that
it can be our thing.

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:54.240
I don't know if there was a moment,

00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:56.141
it's more as I was
thinking about it for this

00:42:56.141 --> 00:42:58.440
and realizing how many movies

00:42:58.440 --> 00:42:59.273
we had seen together,

00:42:59.273 --> 00:43:00.690
and how we'd really connected in that way

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:02.790
and how I've been at the same time

00:43:02.790 --> 00:43:04.530
talking to Jesse about how,

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:05.363
man, you know,

00:43:05.363 --> 00:43:06.480
I take shepherd to school sometimes

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:07.530
and like we can go the whole,

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:09.630
he just wants to listen to music and like,

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:10.740
I'll ask him a couple questions.

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:11.700
He gives me one word answer.

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:13.170
It's typical 14-year-old.

00:43:13.170 --> 00:43:14.220
Yeah.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:16.890
And he's not being a jackass,

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:18.480
he's just like, I'm going to school.

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:19.380
I don't wanna talk about things right now.

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:20.691
Yeah.

00:43:20.691 --> 00:43:22.530
So, no, it's just more like

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:24.210
reflecting on it being like, ah,

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:25.897
we really talk about a lot of things

00:43:25.897 --> 00:43:27.000
and it also,

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:28.800
the tenor of our conversation moves

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:31.839
from a dad trying to be a dad,

00:43:31.839 --> 00:43:34.680
which happens often with me.

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:35.880
Right.

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:37.656
Meaning like, okay,

00:43:37.656 --> 00:43:38.489
well you need to be doing this,

00:43:38.489 --> 00:43:39.672
you need to be doing this.

00:43:39.672 --> 00:43:40.505
Here's some advice here.

00:43:40.505 --> 00:43:42.630
Here's your talking about your schoolwork,

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:43.560
moves us out--

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:44.393
Common interest.

00:43:44.393 --> 00:43:46.050
Of that dynamic into just like

00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:47.790
two guys talking about movies.

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:48.623
Right.

00:43:48.623 --> 00:43:49.710
Which is, which is great for us.

00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:51.330
That's awesome.

00:43:51.330 --> 00:43:54.960
My number seven is my

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:58.860
DJ Elkhound Snuggle Baby journey.

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:00.068
Not that--

00:44:00.068 --> 00:44:00.901
Number seven?

00:44:00.901 --> 00:44:02.730
Not that DJ Elkhound
Snuggle Baby is my name.

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:04.140
It's just Elkhound Snuggle Baby.

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:06.090
But it, I'm just clarifying.

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:07.316
My DJ journey.

00:44:07.316 --> 00:44:08.370
Maybe put it in,

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:10.080
maybe in a second parenthesis

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:11.043
after Snuggle Baby.

00:44:11.043 --> 00:44:12.090
It's just DJ.

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:13.140
'cause you don't want people

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:14.850
to call you DJ Elkhound Snuggle Baby,

00:44:14.850 --> 00:44:16.980
It could be Elkhound Snuggle Baby, DJ.

00:44:16.980 --> 00:44:18.990
I have to,

00:44:18.990 --> 00:44:21.300
I was giving up the opportunity

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:23.940
to DJ our Mythical Christmas party,

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:25.890
because I only have one freaking arm.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:27.030
Yeah.

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:28.530
It's sad, really.

00:44:28.530 --> 00:44:30.390
I don't, but I'm not going to,

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:31.680
I'm still gonna do it.

00:44:31.680 --> 00:44:32.550
Yeah, right.

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:34.849
Because I'll just do it with one arm.

00:44:34.849 --> 00:44:37.031
I'll have, I'll have been in the,

00:44:37.031 --> 00:44:39.660
in the Baby Bjorn in front of me.

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:40.960
Are you going full suit?

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:44.730
I think I have to.

00:44:44.730 --> 00:44:45.563
I haven't--

00:44:45.563 --> 00:44:46.778
You tried that arm with your--

00:44:46.778 --> 00:44:47.611
I haven't thought about it.

00:44:47.611 --> 00:44:49.170
I haven't thought about any of this yet,

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:51.441
but big, I mean,

00:44:51.441 --> 00:44:53.940
it was the year of the genesis

00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:55.860
of Elkhound Snuggle Baby.

00:44:55.860 --> 00:45:00.240
So yeah, that's it.

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:01.500
I've already talked a lot about it.

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:02.970
We dedicated a whole episode to it.

00:45:02.970 --> 00:45:04.470
So let's keep going.

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:05.610
Okay.

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:08.519
Number six for me was getting COVID.

00:45:08.519 --> 00:45:09.987
I thought about

00:45:09.987 --> 00:45:11.598
putting that on the list, and I forgot.

00:45:11.598 --> 00:45:16.181
You know, so many people at this point,

00:45:16.181 --> 00:45:19.735
most people, have gotten it.

00:45:19.735 --> 00:45:21.780
And it has been,

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:23.340
I was actually thinking,

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:24.870
just the other day,

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:27.360
about how we're about
to transition into 2023.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:29.670
And I remember at the very beginning

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:31.350
of all this and,

00:45:31.350 --> 00:45:32.520
March, 2020,

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:34.680
talking to our good friend Mike McCard,

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:35.883
smartest guy I know.

00:45:36.958 --> 00:45:37.920
Thanks.

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:42.920
And he was talking to us about COVID,

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:49.230
at that time and you know, he's smart.

00:45:49.230 --> 00:45:50.550
And so he was like,

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:52.440
we'll still be talking about

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:54.840
and dealing with this directly in 2023.

00:45:54.840 --> 00:45:57.750
And I was like, Mike, you crazy man.

00:45:57.750 --> 00:46:00.030
Now first of all, you know,

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:01.230
there's been so much I, you know,

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:02.550
I get on my soapbox about

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:04.800
being frustrated about the COVID thing

00:46:04.800 --> 00:46:07.260
and COVID denial and that kind of thing.

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:08.093
But one of the things

00:46:08.093 --> 00:46:09.480
that I've thought about recently is

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:12.450
how everything that they said,

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:13.470
everything that the,

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:16.007
the epidemiologist said in 2020,

00:46:16.007 --> 00:46:17.760
essentially happened.

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:19.350
They said that we'd still be talking about

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:20.820
and dealing with this in 2023.

00:46:20.820 --> 00:46:23.040
They said that almost
everyone was going to get it.

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:24.360
They said that it was,

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:25.350
this is the number of people

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:27.330
that were gonna die
within a certain range,

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:28.950
go back and look at what they said.

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:30.150
And every single thing that they said

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:30.983
within those ranges,

00:46:30.983 --> 00:46:32.400
is what ended up happening.

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:34.950
Because these things
operate according to models.

00:46:34.950 --> 00:46:35.790
And it's just interesting.

00:46:35.790 --> 00:46:38.850
It's just an interesting thing to note.

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:39.683
Not every--

00:46:39.683 --> 00:46:40.916
Noted.

00:46:40.916 --> 00:46:42.449
They didn't get everything right,

00:46:42.449 --> 00:46:43.282
but it was just,

00:46:43.282 --> 00:46:45.270
but here we are transitioning into 2023.

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:46.830
We are still dealing with it

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:47.730
and talking about it,

00:46:47.730 --> 00:46:49.380
not nearly to the same degree.

00:46:49.380 --> 00:46:51.510
It's not nearly as disruptive as it was.

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:52.928
And you know,

00:46:52.928 --> 00:46:54.935
all indications are that

00:46:54.935 --> 00:46:56.955
it's sort of dwindling.

00:46:56.955 --> 00:46:59.940
It's becoming, every time
we start talking about it,

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:01.410
it's a little bit less of an issue.

00:47:01.410 --> 00:47:02.880
And then eventually it's just like, yeah,

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:04.621
we don't really talk about it,

00:47:04.621 --> 00:47:05.454
think about this very much.

00:47:05.454 --> 00:47:07.080
Unless you're in a super
high risk group, whatever.

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:08.132
That's great.

00:47:08.132 --> 00:47:09.090
But by the time you got it,

00:47:09.090 --> 00:47:11.160
which is also within a week

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:14.070
of when I got it, totally separately.

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:15.549
Yeah.

00:47:15.549 --> 00:47:19.470
It was, like mentally it felt

00:47:19.470 --> 00:47:20.831
totally different than like

00:47:20.831 --> 00:47:23.160
all the different iterations

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:25.690
of how I thought I would
respond to it beforehand.

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:28.140
Well, I had gotten to this place,

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:29.690
which I think a lot of people have got to,

00:47:29.690 --> 00:47:32.310
which was maybe I'm immune to it.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:33.960
I mean some people are, you know,

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:35.220
some people just don't get it,

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:37.578
just because of some
genetic predisposition.

00:47:37.578 --> 00:47:40.680
And of course that turned
out to not be the case.

00:47:40.680 --> 00:47:42.570
But that ended up being, when I got it,

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:43.980
I got it from my wife

00:47:43.980 --> 00:47:47.550
and then spent a week in a hotel room

00:47:47.550 --> 00:47:49.200
in Chapel Hill with her.

00:47:49.200 --> 00:47:50.680
And that was a very

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:55.590
interesting experience for the both of us,

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:57.000
literally being in a hotel room

00:47:57.000 --> 00:47:58.230
and not leaving the hotel room

00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:00.720
except to go out onto the
balcony of the hotel room.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:02.373
Thank God there was one of those,

00:48:04.020 --> 00:48:05.460
for about five days.

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:07.860
And it just was this,

00:48:07.860 --> 00:48:09.960
I, you know, I have my Oura ring,

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:10.920
which is not a sponsor,

00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:12.690
but we are investors,

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:14.280
so maybe they should be.

00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:16.170
But it gives you all your vitals

00:48:16.170 --> 00:48:17.623
and stuff and you know,

00:48:17.623 --> 00:48:19.519
I go back and I can,

00:48:19.519 --> 00:48:20.352
and you can look at all your data

00:48:20.352 --> 00:48:23.310
and I look at the data for that week

00:48:23.310 --> 00:48:25.800
and I see my temperature of like

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:28.770
103 and a half I think is what I got,

00:48:28.770 --> 00:48:30.060
two nights in a row.

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:32.160
And my, like HRV, you know,

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:34.110
heart rate variability was like super low.

00:48:34.110 --> 00:48:35.910
It was like my readiness score

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:37.904
was like in the thirties.

00:48:37.904 --> 00:48:39.900
It was like the lowest it's ever been.

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:41.580
And it was like, that was a tough,

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:43.050
that was a tough time.

00:48:43.050 --> 00:48:45.450
But it's a time that almost everybody

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.190
has had to experience on some level.

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:49.470
And obviously it's been devastating

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:51.660
and continues to be devastating
for a lot of people.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:53.160
Whether you, you know,

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:54.780
you know somebody who lost their lives

00:48:54.780 --> 00:48:56.340
or you're just somebody who's dealing

00:48:56.340 --> 00:48:57.660
with long COVID.

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:00.934
So it's just my personal

00:49:00.934 --> 00:49:03.750
direct encounter with COVID,

00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:07.860
after having encountered all its effects

00:49:07.860 --> 00:49:09.900
as just a member of society.

00:49:09.900 --> 00:49:12.660
I think that would kind
of crystallize in 2023.

00:49:12.660 --> 00:49:14.070
Thankfully I haven't gotten it again

00:49:14.070 --> 00:49:18.660
and who knows if when
or if I'll get it again.

00:49:18.660 --> 00:49:21.300
But it was memorable.

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:22.133
It was a moment.

00:49:22.133 --> 00:49:25.530
I've lost track of the number

00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:27.090
of boosters that I've
gotten at this point.

00:49:27.090 --> 00:49:28.871
I think it's three.

00:49:28.871 --> 00:49:30.288
Is it four?

00:49:30.288 --> 00:49:32.100
I don't know.

00:49:32.100 --> 00:49:33.603
Yeah, all of them.

00:49:34.890 --> 00:49:36.690
Sign me up, man.

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:37.620
Sign me up.

00:49:37.620 --> 00:49:38.453
I'm in the cult.

00:49:38.453 --> 00:49:40.371
I'd love to get boosted.

00:49:40.371 --> 00:49:41.760
Let's see,

00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:45.660
my number six was the launch

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:47.670
of Dispatchers from Myrtle Beach.

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:49.090
Oh yeah.

00:49:49.090 --> 00:49:52.811
Because as my dad and I talk about

00:49:52.811 --> 00:49:57.030
many times on the podcast itself,

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:00.120
it's just, we're just realizing like, wow,

00:50:00.120 --> 00:50:02.610
it just gives us this new touchpoint

00:50:02.610 --> 00:50:04.891
for our relationship to like, move.

00:50:04.891 --> 00:50:06.650
I would, it's not,

00:50:06.650 --> 00:50:09.870
it's to a new level in some ways

00:50:09.870 --> 00:50:12.150
because it's like the,

00:50:12.150 --> 00:50:15.090
just the frequency and the depth

00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:16.710
of our connection are,

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:19.803
are both like on another level.

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:23.610
But it's also expanded the nature

00:50:23.610 --> 00:50:25.230
of our relationship in a way

00:50:25.230 --> 00:50:28.080
that I don't believe
ever would've happened

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:29.520
if it wasn't for the podcast.

00:50:29.520 --> 00:50:33.390
I mean, to know that every week,

00:50:33.390 --> 00:50:34.693
I'm gonna have it,

00:50:35.826 --> 00:50:37.754
it ends up being like a 45,

00:50:37.754 --> 00:50:39.120
50 minute conversation

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:42.900
that then Logan cuts down to 30 minutes.

00:50:42.900 --> 00:50:43.733
Right.

00:50:43.733 --> 00:50:44.850
'Cause after all my dad

00:50:44.850 --> 00:50:46.440
is in charge of the episode.

00:50:46.440 --> 00:50:47.640
So there's some things

00:50:47.640 --> 00:50:50.253
you just gotta tighten up or maybe remove,

00:50:51.900 --> 00:50:53.400
maybe she removes some of the stuff

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:54.750
that I say too.

00:50:54.750 --> 00:50:55.583
I bet.

00:50:55.583 --> 00:50:56.416
Good.

00:50:57.807 --> 00:50:58.980
Two peas in a pod man.

00:50:58.980 --> 00:50:59.813
But yeah,

00:51:01.235 --> 00:51:03.150
it's like we are
interacting with each other

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:04.923
on different terms.

00:51:06.180 --> 00:51:08.823
It's about having fun,

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:12.840
like high value and having fun

00:51:12.840 --> 00:51:15.150
and also just connecting with each other.

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:18.120
So we find ourselves answering questions

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:20.610
that people have emailed or asking,

00:51:20.610 --> 00:51:23.160
I'm asking him questions like,

00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:26.250
what, so what went into naming me?

00:51:26.250 --> 00:51:27.570
I mean, I know that I'm the third

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:28.403
and you're junior.

00:51:28.403 --> 00:51:29.887
It's like, I didn't,

00:51:29.887 --> 00:51:30.870
I had actually never heard like,

00:51:30.870 --> 00:51:32.910
his thoughts about that.

00:51:32.910 --> 00:51:34.710
Like how long was he thinking about

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:37.563
there being a third before he was even,

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:40.380
you know, thinking about having--

00:51:40.380 --> 00:51:41.822
Was it his idea?

00:51:41.822 --> 00:51:42.655
Having kids.

00:51:42.655 --> 00:51:43.488
Well yeah, he was a junior

00:51:43.488 --> 00:51:44.935
and it was just kind,

00:51:44.935 --> 00:51:46.060
it was kind of similar in how

00:51:46.060 --> 00:51:47.179
I thought about Lincoln,

00:51:47.179 --> 00:51:48.210
but the, it wasn't,

00:51:48.210 --> 00:51:50.040
it wasn't that it was
a revolutionary answer,

00:51:50.040 --> 00:51:53.880
but that it was a conversation

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:55.016
we had never had.

00:51:55.016 --> 00:51:55.849
You'd never talked about it.

00:51:55.849 --> 00:51:57.330
You know, and it's,

00:51:57.330 --> 00:51:58.668
it's one thing to talk about like,

00:51:58.668 --> 00:52:01.200
okay, you talk, you call,

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:02.912
if I call my dad on the phone,

00:52:02.912 --> 00:52:04.447
he'd be like,

00:52:04.447 --> 00:52:06.090
"I had a prostate exam.

00:52:06.090 --> 00:52:07.503
They're doing some tests.

00:52:08.400 --> 00:52:10.080
I decided not to tell you about it

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:11.400
until I got the results

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:12.690
and they're fine."

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:14.049
You know, it's like

00:52:14.049 --> 00:52:14.882
that's how the conversation would've gone.

00:52:14.882 --> 00:52:16.162
Yep.

00:52:16.162 --> 00:52:16.995
But like on the podcast,

00:52:16.995 --> 00:52:17.828
it becomes this, like,

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:20.100
all of that,

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:21.660
the important part of the conversation

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:23.790
still happens that like, oh,

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:24.623
I care about you,

00:52:24.623 --> 00:52:27.270
I wanna make sure that
your prostate's good.

00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:30.390
But then it's also the most fun version

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:31.680
of the conversation that,

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:33.231
that we'll have.

00:52:33.231 --> 00:52:34.532
Yeah.

00:52:34.532 --> 00:52:36.780
You know, it's like seeing him grow.

00:52:36.780 --> 00:52:38.760
And like, there's,

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:40.620
you've seen this from me many times.

00:52:40.620 --> 00:52:41.460
I'll come out of this room

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:44.190
and I'll go back into our office

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:47.012
and if you're in there, I'm just like,

00:52:47.012 --> 00:52:48.930
I'm proud of my dad,

00:52:48.930 --> 00:52:50.160
because he is--

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:50.993
Hmm.

00:52:50.993 --> 00:52:53.790
He's actually, he's developing,

00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:55.920
he's really understanding the parts

00:52:55.920 --> 00:52:57.780
of this that he needs to understand,

00:52:57.780 --> 00:52:59.280
but then continuing to not develop

00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:01.140
in the ways that I don't
want him to develop

00:53:01.140 --> 00:53:02.910
so that it stays like,

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:04.050
entertaining in that right way.

00:53:04.050 --> 00:53:06.360
So it's like, like this,

00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:09.300
the way that he decides to prepare,

00:53:09.300 --> 00:53:11.220
like the things that
he brings to the table,

00:53:11.220 --> 00:53:14.670
like he's fully committing
himself to this.

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:16.020
And I,

00:53:16.020 --> 00:53:20.543
I am surprised at how much of him

00:53:20.543 --> 00:53:23.160
is in me,

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:24.660
that has brought me to this point.

00:53:24.660 --> 00:53:27.600
It's like, as much as I knew it,

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:29.280
I'm still surprised by the extent.

00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:30.892
Now you're getting to experience it.

00:53:30.892 --> 00:53:31.725
The yeah, the extent of it.

00:53:31.725 --> 00:53:32.558
So it's like,

00:53:33.570 --> 00:53:35.220
I was, you know,

00:53:35.220 --> 00:53:36.750
I didn't wanna get my hopes up

00:53:36.750 --> 00:53:39.180
when we decided to do it.

00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:41.610
I was very reticent to like,

00:53:41.610 --> 00:53:44.790
like fully commit to it mentally.

00:53:44.790 --> 00:53:46.159
It's like, oh,

00:53:46.159 --> 00:53:47.700
it's just something
we'll do for a little bit

00:53:47.700 --> 00:53:48.600
and, you know,

00:53:48.600 --> 00:53:49.860
all good things come to an end,

00:53:49.860 --> 00:53:51.420
but like there's no end in sight

00:53:51.420 --> 00:53:53.340
for Dispatchers from Myrtle Beach.

00:53:53.340 --> 00:53:54.173
And I think it,

00:53:54.173 --> 00:53:56.640
it ultimately comes down to the experience

00:53:56.640 --> 00:53:58.950
that we have with each other.

00:53:58.950 --> 00:54:00.631
And then it's up to the,

00:54:00.631 --> 00:54:03.000
it's up to an audience to like

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:05.007
know what they want to be a part of that.

00:54:05.007 --> 00:54:08.154
And like enough people are,

00:54:08.154 --> 00:54:09.270
in my opinion,

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:11.640
the audience still needs to grow.

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:12.473
I would love to,

00:54:12.473 --> 00:54:14.430
I'd love for more people to know about it

00:54:14.430 --> 00:54:15.794
and hear about it.

00:54:15.794 --> 00:54:16.627
And it's challenging to know how

00:54:16.627 --> 00:54:18.453
to get a podcast out there,

00:54:21.540 --> 00:54:23.790
but absolutely on the list,

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:24.873
you know it,

00:54:24.873 --> 00:54:26.550
it could probably be higher,

00:54:26.550 --> 00:54:28.290
but it's my number six.

00:54:28.290 --> 00:54:30.430
He is, he has fully embraced

00:54:32.340 --> 00:54:36.000
his role and that wasn't something

00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:36.833
that was guaranteed.

00:54:36.833 --> 00:54:37.666
No.

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:42.033
My number five,

00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:45.600
is my trip with Locke

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:47.070
from Maine to Miami,

00:54:47.070 --> 00:54:49.474
which we did a whole episode on.

00:54:49.474 --> 00:54:50.307
That's a big one.

00:54:50.307 --> 00:54:52.620
The specific moment from that trip

00:54:52.620 --> 00:54:56.092
is our walk a around
the the Washington Mall.

00:54:56.092 --> 00:54:57.510
Yeah.

00:54:57.510 --> 00:55:00.092
Around the, you know,
the monuments at night.

00:55:00.092 --> 00:55:01.920
And, you know,

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:03.510
because of the time that we recorded

00:55:03.510 --> 00:55:04.500
this episode last year,

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:06.800
we always recorded before
the end of the year,

00:55:07.860 --> 00:55:10.350
technically my other road trip with Locke,

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:13.315
which was in December of 2021,

00:55:13.315 --> 00:55:15.330
when we had to drive back

00:55:15.330 --> 00:55:18.750
from North Carolina to California.

00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:21.982
Those two road trips with Locke,

00:55:21.982 --> 00:55:25.050
I think fundamentally changed

00:55:25.050 --> 00:55:26.970
the dynamic of our relationship,

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:30.870
which fundamentally changed the dynamic

00:55:30.870 --> 00:55:32.722
of our relationship that exists now

00:55:32.722 --> 00:55:34.486
while he is at college, right?

00:55:34.486 --> 00:55:35.319
Yeah.

00:55:35.319 --> 00:55:38.107
So it's funny how,

00:55:38.107 --> 00:55:40.020
and I think this,

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:42.780
it makes me think about these moments

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:44.593
in a different way.

00:55:44.593 --> 00:55:45.426
And 'cause a lot of the things

00:55:45.426 --> 00:55:46.860
that we will list,

00:55:46.860 --> 00:55:48.330
are things that are outside

00:55:48.330 --> 00:55:49.440
of the ordinary experience.

00:55:49.440 --> 00:55:50.960
A lot of the things will be

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:53.828
in our trips that you went on, right?

00:55:53.828 --> 00:55:54.661
Yeah.

00:55:54.661 --> 00:55:55.947
And we talked about this before,

00:55:55.947 --> 00:55:57.810
but doing something,

00:55:57.810 --> 00:56:00.360
and this is why I tell Shepherd,

00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:02.400
you know, go to a summer camp,

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:06.540
like, do these things, go on a trip,

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:08.490
take advantage of this opportunity

00:56:08.490 --> 00:56:10.140
because shaking your life up

00:56:10.140 --> 00:56:12.420
and doing this short term thing.

00:56:12.420 --> 00:56:13.702
Yeah.

00:56:13.702 --> 00:56:14.535
When you look back on your life,

00:56:14.535 --> 00:56:15.825
it'll be the things

00:56:15.825 --> 00:56:17.760
that took you out of your normal routine.

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:19.350
That's what you will remember.

00:56:19.350 --> 00:56:21.420
Those will be the formative experiences.

00:56:21.420 --> 00:56:23.460
Those will be the times in which you like,

00:56:23.460 --> 00:56:25.380
put your mouth up to a fire hydrant

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:28.500
and basically ingested an experience

00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:30.082
at a much higher rate

00:56:30.082 --> 00:56:32.820
than you do in your normal life.

00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:35.340
And those two trips with Locke

00:56:35.340 --> 00:56:36.173
are a testament to it,

00:56:36.173 --> 00:56:37.500
just being in a car together,

00:56:37.500 --> 00:56:38.730
going across the country,

00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:40.230
just the two of us.

00:56:40.230 --> 00:56:41.760
And I think it was a five day trip

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:43.800
and then maybe a five or six day trip.

00:56:43.800 --> 00:56:48.424
So we're talking about
10 to 11 days of time

00:56:48.424 --> 00:56:52.620
that helped define our dynamic

00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:54.480
and I think helped us move

00:56:54.480 --> 00:56:55.530
in the same way that this,

00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:56.580
I'm talking about with Shepherd,

00:56:56.580 --> 00:57:00.480
it moved from so much of this

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:02.430
dad and son dynamic,

00:57:02.430 --> 00:57:04.350
which for me ends up being

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:06.720
the what is my role

00:57:06.720 --> 00:57:08.850
in giving them the best advice

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:11.940
and guiding them, which is important.

00:57:11.940 --> 00:57:13.285
Yeah.

00:57:13.285 --> 00:57:14.580
But moving to that connection,

00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:18.303
which is as a kid becomes an adult,

00:57:18.303 --> 00:57:19.740
goes off to college,

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:20.700
becomes independent,

00:57:20.700 --> 00:57:23.430
the dynamic that you want to continue.

00:57:23.430 --> 00:57:25.240
And the only dynamic
that's worth continuing

00:57:25.240 --> 00:57:27.480
is the connection dynamic,

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:29.040
because the guidance dynamic,

00:57:29.040 --> 00:57:31.170
the parenting dynamic goes away

00:57:31.170 --> 00:57:33.330
and actually in many ways
becomes a burden to them

00:57:33.330 --> 00:57:34.800
as they become adults, right?

00:57:34.800 --> 00:57:36.420
And I see a lot of relationships,

00:57:36.420 --> 00:57:38.010
a lot of parent and child relationships

00:57:38.010 --> 00:57:40.110
that as the child becomes an adult,

00:57:40.110 --> 00:57:41.640
doesn't matter how old they get,

00:57:41.640 --> 00:57:43.620
it doesn't matter how much experience

00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:44.490
they get under their belts,

00:57:44.490 --> 00:57:46.860
it doesn't matter if they
become parents or not.

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:49.410
The dynamic remains a parent and a child.

00:57:49.410 --> 00:57:50.243
Yeah.

00:57:50.243 --> 00:57:51.660
You will always be my child

00:57:51.660 --> 00:57:53.400
and I will always need to guide you

00:57:53.400 --> 00:57:55.350
and I will always need to call you out.

00:57:56.663 --> 00:57:57.570
And that dynamic can be really

00:57:57.570 --> 00:57:59.460
detrimental to relationships.

00:57:59.460 --> 00:58:01.590
And I'm not saying that I engineered this

00:58:01.590 --> 00:58:02.820
or I read a book.

00:58:02.820 --> 00:58:04.917
I got lucky in a lot of ways by just,

00:58:04.917 --> 00:58:07.020
the first trip was circumstance.

00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:08.310
We needed to make a trip

00:58:08.310 --> 00:58:10.792
because the way the plans changed

00:58:10.792 --> 00:58:13.620
and then we, because
that one went so well,

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:15.070
we decided to do another one.

00:58:16.313 --> 00:58:17.668
And those two trips in short succession,

00:58:17.668 --> 00:58:19.380
I think is the reason

00:58:19.380 --> 00:58:22.200
that we talk on the phone all the time now

00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:24.810
in a way that we didn't.

00:58:24.810 --> 00:58:25.643
Huh.

00:58:25.643 --> 00:58:27.390
In a way that we just,

00:58:27.390 --> 00:58:28.890
we didn't communicate with that

00:58:28.890 --> 00:58:30.978
frequency and that depth.

00:58:30.978 --> 00:58:33.363
It's actually, it's increased.

00:58:33.363 --> 00:58:35.397
The timing of it was so crucial.

00:58:35.397 --> 00:58:36.510
And the funny thing is,

